xnu: MERLIN 0098 multi-block eMMC transfers (CMD18/CMD25) to cut boot time

The merlin MSDC root driver issued one CMD17 per 512-byte sector and
measured ~0.7 MB/s: an XNU boot reads ~267 MB of the root volume, i.e.
~7 minutes of the "iOS is preparing" phase.

Merge the read and write paths into msdc_transfer() and use the card's
multi-block commands with the controller's auto CMD12, the same shape the
stock ComboA driver programs for a DMA request (rawcmd dtype 2 << 11,
autocmd12 1 << 28, SDC_BLK_NUM = blocks, MSDC_DMA_LEN = bytes):

  - msdc_read()/the strategy loop move whole sectors in chunks of up to
    128 sectors (64 KiB bounce buffer) through CMD18 reads (CMD25 for
    writes), so an aligned 256 KiB read costs 4 commands instead of 512;
  - single-sector requests, partial heads and tails keep the original
    CMD17/CMD24 shape, including the read-modify-write tail on the write
    side;
  - auto CMD12 keeps the stop inside the data command; the ACMD ready /
    timeout / CRC bits join the wait set and XFER_COMPL is required, as
    the stock driver does ("Autocmd12 issued but error, data transfer
    done INT won't set");
  - the 0085 DMA-canary scan now starts at the requested transfer length
    (a 64 KiB CMD18 legitimately fills the whole bounce buffer);
  - the 0093 readverify double-read and pattern tripwire stay on the
    single-sector path, so msdc_blocks=1 reproduces the old behaviour
    for A/B runs; msdc_blocks=N (1..128) caps the chunk size, printed at
    attach.

